#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Simulate Bose-Hubbard dynamics with a choice of encoding for the latest "
"version of Qiskit bosons"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Simulate Bose-Hubbard dynamics with a choice of encoding"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"bose-hubbard-getting-started\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Simulate Bose-Hubbard dynamics with a choice of encoding {#simulate-bose-"
"hubbard-dynamics-with-a-choice-of-encoding}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This guide builds a circuit for the **time dynamics of the one-dimensional "
"Bose-Hubbard model**, from writing down the Hamiltonian to checking the "
"evolved site densities against an exact reference. What makes the route "
"interesting is a single idea:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"> Do not choose a boson-to-qubit encoding when you build the circuit. Build "
"the circuit on **bosonic modes**, and let the **transpiler** decide how "
"those modes become qubits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For bosons, you nearly always need to defer the encoding. A bosonic mode "
"holds arbitrarily many particles, so its Fock space is infinite-dimensional "
"and no encoding exists until you first pick a **truncation** $d$ (the number"
" of occupation levels you keep). In code the truncation is given as the "
"largest occupation a mode can hold, `max_occupation` $= d - 1$, so a mode "
"that keeps the levels $0, 1, 2$ has `max_occupation = 2` and $d = 3$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"That makes the truncation a *parameter of the encoding*, not of the physics,"
" and it is a parameter you want to vary: too small and the answer is wrong, "
"too large and the circuit is needlessly wide. If you defer the encoding to "
"transpile time, you can sweep the truncation without changing the circuit, "
"the Hamiltonian, or the grouping.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"See also\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  The [operators guide](operators#operators-explanation) for the operator data structure, the [mappers guide](mappers#mappers-explanation) for the encodings and the recipe for writing a custom mapper, and the [transpilation guide](transpilation#transpilation-explanation) for the transpiler stages referenced throughout.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Truncation, and what it costs {#truncation-and-what-it-costs}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"When you truncate a mode to $d$ levels, you break the canonical commutator "
"on the highest retained level <span id=\"id1\" />[\\[1\\]](#id3), so no "
"encoding is an isomorphism of *algebras*: a bosonic encoding maps states, "
"and operators acting within the truncated subspace. Two consequences matter "
"for everything that follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1.  '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$d$ must bound the occupation reachable by the dynamics, not merely that of "
"the initial state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2.  '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Mapping does not commute with normal ordering, so decide once whether your "
"Hamiltonian is defined in normal-ordered form, and stay consistent.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The [mappers guide](mappers#truncation-explanation) derives the defect and "
"shows both consequences on running code.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"the-hamiltonian\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. The Hamiltonian {#1-the-hamiltonian}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Bose-Hubbard model on a lattice with bonds $\\langle i j \\rangle$ is\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"H = -t \\sum_{\\langle i j \\rangle} \\left(b^\\dagger_i b_j + b^\\dagger_j b_i\\right)\n"
"    + \\frac{U}{2} \\sum_i n_i (n_i - 1) \\, ,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"with $n_i = b^\\dagger_i b_i$. The interaction counts *pairs* of bosons on a"
" site, so it requires a site to hold two particles at once. Normal-ordered, "
"$n_i(n_i-1) = b^\\dagger_i b^\\dagger_i b_i b_i$, which is the form used in "
"what follows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The geometry enters only through the edge list, so nothing is specific to a "
"chain:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Two properties are worth checking first. The Hamiltonian must be Hermitian "
"for $e^{-itH}$ to be unitary, and it conserves the total boson number:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Now the bosonic question: how many levels does this operator demand?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The interaction term applies two creation operators to one mode, so a mode "
"has to hold two bosons for the term to act at all. That is a **maximum "
"occupation** of 2, which is the number that "
"[`LevelSpec`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/mappers-level-"
"spec#qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelSpec \"qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelSpec\") "
"takes as its `max_occupation`. A mode that keeps the occupations $0, 1, 2$ "
"has $d = \\texttt{max\\_occupation} + 1 = 3$ levels, so `max_occupation >= "
"2` is the same requirement as $d \\geq 3$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is a lower bound only: it comes from one application of the operator, "
"and repeated application over a time evolution can reach further. It rules "
"out cutoffs that are certainly too small, and step 6 settles, for this model"
" and state, how large the cutoff has to be. "
"[`mode_occupations()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-boson-"
"operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.mode_occupations "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.mode_occupations\") reports the "
"bound for each mode separately, which is what a per-mode cutoff is compared "
"against.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"group-before-mapping\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"bose-hubbard-grouping\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Group before mapping {#2-group-before-mapping}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The evolution is Trotterized, and how the Hamiltonian is partitioned decides"
" what the synthesis is handed. Two considerations pull in the same "
"direction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First, a physical one: the individual hopping term $b^\\dagger_i b_j$ is "
"**not Hermitian**. Only the pair $b^\\dagger_i b_j + b^\\dagger_j b_i$ is. "
"If you evolve under a single non-Hermitian term, the operation is not "
"unitary, and the mapped operator would carry complex coefficients that "
"[`PauliEvolutionGate`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.circuit.library.PauliEvolutionGate)"
" rejects. Each bond’s two hopping terms must therefore stay together.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Second, an efficiency one: all the on-site interaction terms are diagonal in"
" the occupation basis and mutually commute, so they can share a single group"
" and be synthesized together. The same holds for the bonds that touch no "
"common site. On a chain, every second bond is such a set: bonds 0 and 2 act "
"on disjoint pairs of modes, so their hopping terms commute and can share a "
"group, and bond 1 forms a group of its own. If you group the even and the "
"odd bonds, you therefore need three groups, where one group per bond needs "
"four. Fewer groups mean fewer factors, and also a shallower circuit: with "
"the Gray code at `max_occupation = 2` the two-qubit depth is 95 against 139."
" The lattice decides which bonds can share a group, which is why the "
"partition is chosen here.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The [`groups`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"boson-operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.groups "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.groups\") attribute carries that "
"decision into the circuit: an "
"[`Evolution`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/circuit-"
"library-evolution#qiskit_bosons.circuit.library.Evolution "
"\"qiskit_bosons.circuit.library.Evolution\") gate over a grouped operator "
"decomposes into one sub-evolution per group. The partition is chosen at the "
"bosonic level, while the lattice structure is still visible, and it survives"
" untouched through the mapping. The [grouping guide](grouping#grouping-"
"explanation) covers the checks a grouping should pass.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every group must itself be Hermitian, which is what the preceding pairing "
"buys. [`groups_are_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-terms-groups-are-"
"hermitian#qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.groups_are_hermitian "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.groups_are_hermitian\") checks all groups in"
" one call:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To see what each group holds, separate the groups:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The even-and-odd grouping is a choice, and the alternative is easy to "
"measure. If you give every bond a group of its own, you need four groups, "
"and the same circuit then costs more two-qubit depth. The depth is counted "
"after the circuit is lowered with the Gray code at `max_occupation = 2`, "
"which step 4 explains:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"the-bosonic-circuit\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. The bosonic circuit {#3-the-bosonic-circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Note what is absent from the circuit. There is no truncation, no encoding, "
"and no qubit count. It acts on four bosonic modes:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A BosonicCircuit on four modes with an InitializeModes gate followed by "
"one Evolution gate.](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-"
"bosons/bose_hubbard-9.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The wires are bosonic modes, not qubits. Note that "
"[`InitializeModes`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/circuit-"
"library-initialize-modes#qiskit_bosons.circuit.library.InitializeModes "
"\"qiskit_bosons.circuit.library.InitializeModes\") takes **integers**, not "
"flags: a mode can hold any number of bosons, so a Fock state is a list of "
"occupation numbers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A single [`decompose()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/circuit-bosonic-"
"circuit#qiskit_bosons.circuit.BosonicCircuit.decompose "
"\"qiskit_bosons.circuit.BosonicCircuit.decompose\") turns the grouping from "
"[step 2](#bose-hubbard-grouping) into one gate per group:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![The decomposed BosonicCircuit with one Evolution gate per "
"group.](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-bosons/bose_hubbard-11.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The first group holds the bonds on modes 0 and 1 and on modes 2 and 3, so "
"its gate spans all four modes, and so does the on-site group. The middle "
"bond has a gate of its own on modes 1 and 2.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Skip that decomposition and the grouping is simply ignored. Which method "
"does the splitting is the `synthesis` of the gate, with "
"[`BosonicLieTrotter`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/circuit-library-synthesis-bosonic-lie-"
"trotter#qiskit_bosons.circuit.library.synthesis.BosonicLieTrotter "
"\"qiskit_bosons.circuit.library.synthesis.BosonicLieTrotter\") as the "
"default; [Synthesize an Evolution gate in bosonic space](bosonic-"
"synthesis#bosonic-synthesis-explanation) shows the alternatives.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"choose-the-encoding-at-transpile-time\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"bose-hubbard-transpile\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"4. Choose the encoding at transpile time {#4-choose-the-encoding-at-"
"transpile-time}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here is the payoff. The same circuit is lowered four times, under four "
"different [`LevelEncoding`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/mappers-level-encoding#qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelEncoding "
"\"qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelEncoding\") values, by changing one argument. A"
" [`LevelSpec`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/mappers-level-"
"spec#qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelSpec \"qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelSpec\") "
"pairs the encoding with the truncation of every mode, and the following "
"preset derives the layout, the state preparation, and the operator mapping "
"from that one spec (see [Describe the truncation with a "
"LevelSpec](mappers#level-spec-explanation)). A string such as `\"gray\"` is "
"accepted wherever a `LevelEncoding` is.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"These numbers contradict a natural guess. The unary encodings use $d$ qubits"
" per mode against $\\lceil \\log_2 d \\rceil$ for the compact codes, and at "
"$d = 3$ the compact codes win on both axes at once: fewer qubits and far "
"fewer entangling gates. The two unary variants differ in the operator they "
"emit, not in their layout: "
"[`LevelEncoding.UNARY_PROJECTED`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/mappers-level-"
"encoding#qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelEncoding.UNARY_PROJECTED "
"\"qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelEncoding.UNARY_PROJECTED\") carries a projector"
" on every other qubit of a mode, which makes it the most expensive of the "
"four to evolve, and "
"[`LevelEncoding.UNARY_UNPROJECTED`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/mappers-level-"
"encoding#qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelEncoding.UNARY_UNPROJECTED "
"\"qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelEncoding.UNARY_UNPROJECTED\") leaves the "
"projectors out and agrees with it on the one-hot subspace. The [mappers "
"guide](mappers#unary-variants-explanation) shows the two operators side by "
"side, and explains why the Gray code beats standard binary (SB) on the "
"hopping terms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sawaya et al. report the same ordering <span id=\"id2\" />[\\[2\\]](#id4):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"> though the unary code tends to require more qubits but fewer operations, "
"it is often the case that the SB or Gray code is more efficient both in "
"terms of qubit counts and operation counts\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Their conclusion is that the best code depends on the Hamiltonian and on "
"$d$, which is the strongest argument for not fixing the choice in the "
"circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Note\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Under a level encoding a mode spans several qubits, so the qubit register is wider than the mode register. The preset uses [`OccupationB2QLayout`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/transpiler-passes-occupation-b-2-q-layout#qiskit_bosons.transpiler.passes.OccupationB2QLayout \"qiskit_bosons.transpiler.passes.OccupationB2QLayout\"), which derives the register width from the [`LevelSpec`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/mappers-level-spec#qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelSpec \"qiskit_bosons.mappers.LevelSpec\"). The [transpilation guide](transpilation#transpilation-explanation) walks through the stages the preset runs.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"verify-against-exact-dynamics\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"bose-hubbard-verify\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "5. Verify against exact dynamics {#5-verify-against-exact-dynamics}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"None of the preceding steps establishes that the circuit is right. For that,"
" compare the site densities $\\langle n_m \\rangle$ after evolution against "
"an exact reference computed on the encoded Hamiltonian.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The reference has to be computed in the same truncated space the circuit "
"works in, because comparing against an untruncated calculation would "
"conflate Trotter error with truncation error, which are different things and"
" worth separating (see [Truncate the Fock space](mappers#truncation-"
"explanation)).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "With those in hand, the exact evolution in the truncated space:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The bosons spread from the occupied end of the chain towards the empty one, "
"and the two bosons are conserved:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now the circuit. Note that the preceding reference was built from `initial` "
"directly, so the circuit compared against it must contain only the "
"evolution, because the "
"[`InitializeModes`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/circuit-"
"library-initialize-modes#qiskit_bosons.circuit.library.InitializeModes "
"\"qiskit_bosons.circuit.library.InitializeModes\") gate would re-prepare a "
"state that is already prepared.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The error halves as the step count doubles, which is the "
"$O(t^2/\\text{steps})$ signature of a first-order product formula. That is "
"the evidence that the discrepancy is Trotter error and the encoding is "
"faithful.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  The repetitions come from the `synthesis` of the gate, which Trotterizes in bosonic space where the grouping still exists: each step applies every group once, and the steps follow one another. That is different from handing a [`LieTrotter`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.synthesis.LieTrotter) with `reps > 1` to the qubit-level synthesis plugin. There, each group is already its own [`PauliEvolutionGate`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.circuit.library.PauliEvolutionGate), so the formula only subdivides the terms within a group and never interleaves the groups. The groups do not mutually commute, so that leaves a single outer Trotter step whose error does not shrink however large `reps` is. [Synthesize an Evolution gate in bosonic space](bosonic-synthesis#bosonic-synthesis-explanation) compares the two and covers higher orders.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Warning\" type=\"caution\">\n"
"  The preceding [`decompose()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.circuit.QuantumCircuit#decompose) call is essential. [`evolve()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.quantum_info.Statevector#evolve) uses a [`PauliEvolutionGate`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.circuit.library.PauliEvolutionGate)’s exact definition and bypasses the product formula entirely. If you verify against an un-decomposed circuit, you see near-perfect agreement even for a deliberately wrong synthesis. The un-decomposed circuit also forces a dense $2^{n} \\times 2^{n}$ operator to be built, whereas a decomposed circuit applies gates one at a time. Other simulators can treat the gate differently, so this check depends on the simulator, whereas hardware always needs the decomposed form.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Finally, the central check: the physics must not depend on which encoding "
"was chosen.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The qubit counts, Pauli structures, and circuit depths all differ, and the "
"physics agrees to ten decimal places. The encoding is an implementation "
"choice, which is why it belongs to the transpiler. The two unary variants "
"agree here because the simulated state stays on the one-hot subspace; away "
"from it they would differ.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"truncation-is-a-convergence-parameter\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"6. Truncation is a convergence parameter {#6-truncation-is-a-convergence-"
"parameter}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The final step is specific to bosons. Everything so far had fixed "
"`max_occupation = 2`, that is $d = 3$. However, the truncation is not a free"
" choice: it is a **convergence parameter**, and an answer is only "
"trustworthy after it stops moving (see [Truncate the Fock "
"space](mappers#truncation-explanation)).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The circuit carries no truncation, so the circuit itself does not change "
"when the cutoff does: only the transpilation is repeated. What changes is "
"the size of what comes out, and a sweep is only as cheap as it is here "
"because the system is tiny. The following reference is an exact classical "
"simulation of the truncated dynamics, which is possible for four sites and "
"grows exponentially with the number of qubits. For a problem beyond that "
"reach there is no exact reference to compare against, and the convergence "
"has to be judged by other means.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"At `max_occupation = 1` ($d = 2$) each site holds at most one boson, so the "
"interaction term, which needs two on a site, cannot act at all, and the "
"densities are off by about $7 \\times 10^{-4}$. When you add the third "
"level, the result captures the interaction, and from `max_occupation = 3` on"
" the result is stationary to machine precision: converged.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The reason that it stops at `max_occupation = 2` is particle number. The "
"Hamiltonian conserves the number of bosons, and this state holds two, so no "
"site can ever hold more than two, whatever the evolution time and whether "
"the interaction is repulsive or attractive. If you evolve to $t = 6$ instead"
" of $t = 0.4$, or flip the sign of $U$, the densities at `max_occupation = "
"2` and higher stay unchanged to $10^{-13}$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"What does push the requirement up is the **number of bosons**. Three bosons "
"on the same chain change by about $2 \\times 10^{-3}$ between "
"`max_occupation = 2` and `3` after the same short evolution, and by about "
"$0.3$ after $t = 6$. That is a statement about this model and a conserved "
"quantity, and it is a bound only for operators that conserve the particle "
"number; it is not a general rule, so the cutoff has to be swept, not "
"guessed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The sweep has a price on the circuit side too, which is what the exact "
"reference does not show. The same bosonic circuit, lowered with the Gray "
"code at each cutoff:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Each extra level costs depth, and the qubit count jumps whenever $d$ passes "
"a power of two. When you converge in $d$, you therefore trade accuracy "
"against circuit size, so the sweep is more than a numerical check.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Note also that $d = 2$ being nearly right is a coincidence of the "
"observable: the site *densities* barely move, while the underlying state is "
"qualitatively wrong (it has no doubly-occupied component). If one observable"
" converges, that does not show that another has converged.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What to take away {#what-to-take-away}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The encoding is a transpiler concern.** The bosonic circuit in [step "
"3](#bose-hubbard-transpile) mentions no truncation, no encoding, and no "
"qubit count. Four encodings then lowered it to four different circuits, all "
"reproducing the same physics. Nothing upstream of the transpiler had to "
"change.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**For bosons this is not merely convenient.** The truncation is a "
"convergence parameter that has to be swept, and it lives inside the "
"encoding. A circuit built against a fixed encoding would have to be rebuilt "
"for every value of $d$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Group before mapping, and group for Hermiticity.** A single hopping term "
"is not Hermitian and cannot be exponentiated; the bond pair on the hand is "
"Hermitian and therefore can be exponentiated. Decide the partition at the "
"bosonic level, where the lattice is still visible. That is physically "
"necessary, and the [`groups`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-boson-operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.groups"
" \"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.groups\") attribute exists to carry"
" the decision forward.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Do not assume the low-weight encoding is the cheap one.** At $d = 3$ the "
"compact codes beat both unary variants on qubits and gates simultaneously, "
"and the projected variant is the costliest of all. Measure; the crossover is"
" $d$-dependent.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Truncation breaks the algebra, in a quantifiable way.** Only on the top "
"retained level, by $d$. That is also why mapping does not commute with "
"normal ordering.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Next steps {#next-steps}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Two directions extend this example. If you replace the chain’s edge list "
"with the bonds of a square lattice, you need no other change, because the "
"`bose_hubbard` function defined earlier is already geometry-agnostic. A "
"custom encoding of your own (a block-unary code, for example, which "
"interpolates between unary and compact) also plugs in through the same "
"`MapperFnEvolutionSynthesis` seam the preset uses.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Design principles of operator representations](operators#operators-"
"explanation) explains the operator data structure behind the Hamiltonian.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Map operator representations](mappers#mappers-explanation) covers the "
"encodings and writing a mapper of your own.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Transpile bosonic circuits](transpilation#transpilation-explanation) walks "
"through the stages the preset runs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Synthesize an Evolution gate in bosonic space](bosonic-synthesis#bosonic-"
"synthesis-explanation) shows how to reduce the Trotter error in bosonic "
"space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  Every depth reported is on an abstract, fully connected register. Mapping to real hardware connectivity is a separate stage, and an occupation encoding’s per-mode qubit blocks give a router rather different structure to work with than one qubit per mode.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

